# PATCH: 026-restrict-remote-commands
#
# PURPOSE: Hold command lines forwarded to a running browser to the launcher's
#          grammar (patch 015). The remote window accepts WM_COPYDATA from any
#          process running as the same user and ran whatever command line it
#          received, so -new-window, -chrome, -file, -url with any scheme and
#          Firefox's other command-line handlers were reachable without going
#          through the launcher.
#
# BEHAVIOR:
#   - A v3 message runs only if it passes patch 015's launch grammar
#     (IsAllowedBrowserCommandLine): <binary> or <binary> -osint -url
#     http(s)://..., the shapes a second, launcher-approved DenBrowser
#     instance forwards. Every argument must be valid UTF-8. den: links are
#     refused; the forwarding instance's launcher has already unwrapped them.
#   - v2 messages (ESR 128's format, never sent by DenBrowser) are refused.
#   - The sender's working directory is ignored, so -url is never resolved
#     against a path the sender chose (such as a UNC share).
#   - A refused message runs nothing.
#
# TESTS: TestDenBrowserLaunchPolicy (patch 015) covers the shared grammar.
#        The WinRemoteMessage gtests now expect v2 messages and out-of-grammar
#        v3 messages to be refused and the working directory to be ignored.
#
# LIMITATIONS: This closes the command-line route only. A same-user process can
#              still drive the browser's windows with synthetic input or UI
#              Automation; this patch does not make the user session a
#              security boundary. The sending side is unchanged too: a
#              second instance finds the running browser by its window
#              class name, so a same-user process that registers that
#              class first receives later launches instead.


